  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Notably warm low-level thicknesses beneath \nanomalous ridging aloft will support above to well above normal \ntemperatures for much of ENC through Friday. The one exception \nis the northern OBX where a slowing front may briefly dip into \nthe area leading to a \"cooler\" northeasterly flow. In that area,\nI went below blended guidance to reflect highs not getting \nquite as warm as the rest of ENC where southerly flow will \npersist. \n\nIncreasing low-level moisture will support increasing humidity \non Friday, which should translate to heat indices topping out at\n100- 105 degrees. A Heat Advisory was considered, but the risk \narea appears too small to warrant any headlines for now. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...On Friday, a slowing cold front will begin \nencroaching on ENC from Virginia. Guidance is mixed regarding \nhow far south this front gets before stalling. Given the \nstrength of the Southeast U.S. ridge, it would make sense that \nthe front won't make much headway through ENC. Meanwhile, \nmoisture and instability will continue to increase south of the \nfront, and this should support at least a slight step up in the \nrisk of afternoon convection compared to today. This may \nespecially be the case as a shortwave moves through the area and\nas we get a glancing influence from the entrance region of an \nupper jet that will be sliding by to the north. While the focus \nof convection should be where the front stalls, it stands to \nreason that isolated to scattered convection will be able to \ndevelop, likely focused along the seabreeze. Instability looks \nto be modest (around 1000j/kg MLCAPE), but with some residually \ndry air aloft and higher DCAPE, the strongest storms may produce\ngusty winds of 40-50 mph. \n\nMoisture continues to build on Saturday, with PWATs forecast to\nrise above 2\". This combined with the seabreeze, a dampening \nshortwave, and inland troughing should support an increased \ncoverage of convection compared to Friday. There's some \nuncertainty regarding the strength of convergence along the \ninland trough. If the inland trough ends up weaker, the coverage\nof convection may be lower across the coastal plain. \nRegardless, any thunderstorms that form on Saturday will be \ncapable of very heavy rainfall rates and minor, nuisance-stlye \nflooding. Instability continues to look modest, and this should \nkeep the risk of strong to severe thunderstorms LOW. \n\nOn Sunday, the stalled front should finally get shoved south \nthrough all of ENC as a stronger shortwave passes by to the \nnorth. A continued moist and modestly unstable environment \nshould support another day of scattered to perhaps numerous \nshowers and thunderstorms along the advancing front. Heavy rain \nand minor flooding continues to look like the primary risk.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Anomalous upper level ridging and a notably \nstrong surface high builds into the Mid-Atlantic and Carolinas \nnext week favoring warm and dry conditions.",
